      text: `In our book <em>Führung mit Fundament</em> (which I wrote together with my son David L. Rathmer) we describe a leader who cuts off concerns about a new shift plan directly: "We need to stay capable of acting. We can look at feelings later." The room freezes, the team responds with adaptation, fear, inner withdrawal – she herself interprets this as stability. The turning point comes through a facilitator's question: "What happens here when someone disagrees?" Silence. Then: "You think twice about it." For the first time she recognizes: "Maybe I've confused strength with invulnerability."<br><br>Translated for the SE8, this means: control instead of trust, toughness instead of vulnerability, power instead of contact – these are genuine strengths, but they can paralyze systems without real relationship. The simple sentence "I'll hear you out" – followed by real listening – often opens more than any quick decision.`,
      hinweis: `In the book we describe this case at the level of base type 8, not subtype-specifically. The self-preservation coloring – the control shows up above all in a concrete, practical territory, not in social stage presence or exclusive intensity – is an addition based on the other sources, not a literal quote.`,
